      paragraphs: [
        `CLS measures how much visible content moves unexpectedly while the page loads, and the target is 0.1 or less. It is the most fixable of the three, because nearly every cause is a missing instruction rather than a difficult trade-off.`,
        `The standard causes, in rough order of frequency. Images and videos without width and height attributes, so the browser reserves no space and everything below jumps when they arrive. Content injected above the fold after first paint: a cookie or consent banner, a promotional ribbon, a currency prompt, an announcement bar. Ads and embedded widgets in a container with no reserved height. A webfont that swaps in with different metrics from the fallback and reflows every line of text. And a layout that is assembled by JavaScript after paint, which shifts the whole page at once.`,
        `The fixes are mechanical. Put explicit dimensions or an aspect ratio on every image, video and iframe, so space is reserved before the asset arrives. Reserve height for anything injected: if a banner may appear at the top, lay the page out as though it is already there. Give ad and widget slots a fixed minimum height even when empty. Avoid inserting anything above existing content after first paint; put it at the bottom, or overlay it rather than pushing content down.`,
        `Fonts get their own treatment, which the next section covers in detail, because the font-driven shift is the one that hurts bilingual sites most and is the least understood.`,
        `Two measurement notes that save arguments. CLS is collected across the whole page lifespan in field data, so a shift caused by something that loads lazily when a visitor scrolls counts even though it never appears in a lab run of the initial load. And a layout that is stable on a desktop viewport frequently is not on a phone, where a wrapped headline or a stacked column changes which elements move. Check both, and check the Arabic version separately.`,
      ],
